## Why This Is Asked
Tests partition DP with a subarray range cost, requiring precomputation of subarray extrema and careful reconstruction. It adds a different objective than prior questions by using (max-min) rather than a function of endpoints or a fixed multiplier. 

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions with exact-k segments
- Subarray range queries (max-min) via precomputation
- Path reconstruction from parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs for small to mid-size n

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const maxv = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  const minv = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    maxv[i][i] = A[i];
    minv[i][i] = A[i];
    for (let j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
      maxv[i][j] = Math.max(maxv[i][j-1], A[j]);
      minv[i][j] = Math.min(minv[i][j-1], A[j]);
    }
  }
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = (maxv[0][i-1]-minv[0][i-1]) * i, par[i][1] = 0;
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let p = t-1; p <= i-1; p++) {
        const cost = (maxv[p][i-1]-minv[p][i-1]) * (i-p);
        if (dp[p][t-1] + cost < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[p][t-1] + cost;
          par[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let idx = n, rem = k, cuts = [];
  while (rem > 0) {
    const p = par[idx][rem];
    cuts.push([p, idx-1]);
    idx = p; rem--;
  }
  cuts.reverse();
  return {cost: dp[n][k], blocks: cuts};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This problem evaluates the ability to design a dynamic programming solution for selecting exactly k vertex-disjoint directed paths in a DAG while maximizing total node weights. It is a common pattern in interviews at Netflix, Uber, and Meta.

## Key Concepts

- **Topological ordering** ensures DP processes vertices in valid dependency order
- **DP over path count dimension** (t from 1 to k) tracks the maximum weight for t paths ending at each vertex
- **Vertex-disjoint constraint** is naturally enforced through the state definition, since each path is built incrementally
- **Parent pointer array** enables reconstruction of the selected paths after DP computation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code for k vertex-disjoint path selection

function selectKPaths(N, M, edges, weights, k) {
  const dp = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  // Get topological order
  const topo = topologicalSort(N, edges);

  // Initialize sources
  for (const s of sources) {
    dp[s][1] = weights[s];
  }

  // Process in topological order
  for (const u of topo) {
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      for (let t = 1; t < k; t++) {
        if (dp[u][t] > -Infinity) {
          const newWeight = dp[u][t] + weights[v];
          if (newWeight > dp[v][t + 1]) {
            dp[v][t + 1] = newWeight;
            parent[v][t + 1] = u;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct paths using parent pointers
  return reconstructPaths(dp, parent, k);
}
```

