## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests a foundational dynamic programming pattern—selecting k non-adjacent elements with maximum sum. It assesses understanding of state definition, transition logic, and reconstruction, making it a classic beginner DP problem.

## Key Concepts
- **State definition**: dp[i][j] represents the optimal sum for the first i elements with exactly j selections
- **Transition logic**: Compare skipping the current element vs. taking it (with the appropriate previous state)
- **Backtracking**: Reconstruct chosen indices by comparing states during backtracking
- **Feasibility constraint**: Maximum selections possible in i elements is floor((i+1)/2)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumNonAdjacent(A, m) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(m + 1).fill(NEG));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j <= Math.min(m, Math.floor((i + 1) / 2)); j++) {
      const take = A[i - 1] + (i >= 2 ? dp[i - 2][j - 1] : (j - 1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG));
      const skip = dp[i - 1][j];
      dp[i][j] = Math.max(skip, take);
    }
  }

  const result = dp[n][m];
  const indices = [];
  let i = n, j = m;

  while (i > 0 && j > 0) {
    const take = A[i - 1] + (i >= 2 ? dp[i - 2][j - 1] : (j - 1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG));
    if (dp[i][j] === take) {
      indices.push(i - 1);
      j--;
      i -= 2;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }

  return { sum: result, indices: indices.reverse() };
}
```

**Time Complexity:** O(n × m)
**Space Complexity:** O(n × m), or O(m) with optimization.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model budgeted multi-interval optimization where a fixed daily cost incentivizes shorter campaigns. Requires stateful DP with reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- Up to k non-overlapping subarrays
- Per-element cost transformation: B[i] = A[i] - c
- DP states: end[i][t], best[i][t]
- Recurrences: end[i][t] = max(end[i-1][t] + B[i], best[i-1][t-1] + B[i]); best[i][t] = max(best[i-1][t], end[i][t])
- Reconstruction via backpointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarraysWithCost(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const B = A.map(x => x - c);
  const end = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const best = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prevEnd = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(null));

  best[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    end[i][0] = -Infinity;
    best[i][0] = 0;
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const extend = end[i-1][t] + B[i-1];
      const startNew = best[i-1][t-1] + B[i-1];
      end[i][t] = Math.max(extend, startNew);
      if (best[i-1][t] >= end[i][t]) {
        best[i][t] = best[i-1][t];
      } else {
        best[i][t] = end[i][t];
        prevEnd[i][t] = true;
      }
    }
  }

  let bestVal = -Infinity, bestT = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
    if (best[n][t] > bestVal) { bestVal = best[n][t]; bestT = t; }
  }
  return {profit: bestVal, segments: []};
}
```

